@h1 Rendering h1@

Rendering is the last mile and the most configuration-light stage. It turns an
@code ExpandedDocument code@ into a string — HTML or Markdown — with a pure tree
walk: no I/O, no state beyond the options you pass. Know the two knobs (mode and
CSS) and the fixed AST-to-HTML mapping and you know the whole job.

@h2 HTML h2@

@@@code renderHtml(doc: ExpandedDocument, options?: RenderHtmlOptions) → string code@@@.

@code RenderHtmlOptions code@:

@ul
@li @code mode code@ — @@@code 'fragment' | 'document' code@@@.
@strong Defaults to strong@ @code 'fragment' code@. @code fragment code@ emits
just @@@code <article class="wit-doc">…</article> code@@@; @code document code@
wraps it in a full @code <!doctype html> code@ page with the stylesheet inlined
into @code <head> code@. li@
@li @code title?: string code@ — the document-mode @code <title> code@ (default
@code 'Wit document' code@). li@
@li @code lang?: string code@ — the document-mode @@@code <html lang> code@@@
(default @code 'en' code@). li@
@li @code css?: string code@ — the document-mode stylesheet, inlined into
@code <head> code@. Defaults to @code defaultThemeCss code@. Pass @code '' code@
for an unstyled page, or your own string. li@
ul@

@aside(class note) ⚠️ @code mode code@ defaults to @code 'fragment' code@, not
@code 'document' code@. If you want a standalone HTML file, pass
@@@code { mode: 'document' } code@@@ explicitly. The CLI's default is the
opposite — @code wit build code@ emits a document — so the two surfaces disagree
by design. aside@

@h2 Theming h2@

Two stylesheet strings ship from @@@code @witlang/render-html code@@@:

@ul
@li @code defaultThemeCss code@ — the Word-like house style: CSS custom
properties, a @code prefers-color-scheme code@ dark mode, print and
@@@code @page code@@@ rules. A document with no CSS of its own renders like a
word-processor page. li@
@li @code rawThemeCss code@ — a mechanical reset only (box model, no body margin,
responsive media). You style everything yourself via @@@code @@style code@@@ and
wrapping nodes. li@
ul@

@@@pre
import { renderHtml, defaultThemeCss, rawThemeCss } from '@witlang/render-html';

renderHtml(expanded, { mode: 'document' });                    // house style
renderHtml(expanded, { mode: 'document', css: rawThemeCss });  // bare reset
renderHtml(expanded, { mode: 'document', css: myOwnCss });     // your CSS
renderHtml(expanded, { mode: 'document', css: '' });           // no <style> at all
pre@@@

@h2 The AST-to-HTML mapping h2@

The walk is fixed. The mappings that matter:

@ul
@li @code Paragraph code@ → @code <p> code@; empty paragraphs are dropped, so no
stray @code <p></p> code@. li@
@li @code Italic code@ → @code <em> code@; @code Bold code@ → @code <strong> code@;
@code Text code@ is escaped through @code escapeHtml code@. li@
@li Core vocab maps to its fixed element: @@@code @h1 code@@@ → @code <h1> code@,
@@@code @a |href …| code@@@ → @@@code <a href=…> code@@@, @@@code @img code@@@ →
@code <img> code@, @@@code @row code@@@ / @@@code @col code@@@ → flex
@code <div> code@s, @@@code @br code@@@ / @@@code @hr code@@@ → void elements. li@
@li @@@code @table code@@@ → its dedicated renderer (schema, rows, caption; three
call forms). li@
@li @@@code @bibliography code@@@ → @@@code <div class="wit-bibliography"> code@@@
with one @code <p> code@ per entry. li@
@li An opaque @@@code @node code@@@ → dispatched on its @code type code@ param
(see @a |href /docs/build/custom-nodes/| Custom nodes a@). li@
@li A definition body that collapsed to a literal @code Record code@ →
@@@code <table class="wit-record"> code@@@; a @code Collection code@ →
@@@code <ul class="wit-collection"> code@@@. li@
@li A surviving unknown user @@@code @name code@@@ (rare) →
@@@code <div|span class="wit-node" data-wit-name="…"> code@@@ with each param as
a @code data-* code@ attribute. li@
@li An unresolved @@@code ::x:: code@@@ interpolation →
@@@code <span class="wit-unresolved">::x::</span> code@@@; a surviving unresolved
access @@@code @x.y code@@@ →
@@@code <span class="wit-unresolved">@x.y</span> code@@@ — visible, never silent. li@
ul@

@h2 Safety h2@

All text and attribute values pass through @code escapeHtml code@ (@code ' code@
→ @code &#39; code@, @code " code@ → @code &quot; code@), so malformed prose can
never produce malformed HTML. The raw-text elements @@@code @@style code@@@ and
@@@code @@script code@@@ emit their body @strong verbatim strong@ — CSS and JS
must not be escaped — but the renderer guards the closing tag
(@@@code </style code@@@ becomes @@@code <\/style code@@@ inside the body):

@@@pre
@@style .x{color:red} style@@
// → <style>.x { color: red; }</style>
pre@@@

@aside(class note) 💡 @code WIT_DEBUG_COMMENTS=1 code@ keeps @code Comment code@
nodes as HTML comment markers (default: dropped). @code ScriptBlock code@ and
@code ScriptCall code@ are @strong always strong@ dropped — their effects
already ran during @code expand code@. aside@

@h2 Markdown h2@

@@@code renderMarkdown(doc: ExpandedDocument) → string code@@@ is the single
export — @strong no options. strong@ It is CommonMark-ish, hand-rolled per node
name. The same source rendered both ways:

@@@pre
@h1 Title h1@

Some *bold* and _italic_ prose.

@ul
@li One li@
@li Two li@
ul@
pre@@@

→ Markdown:

@@@pre
# Title

Some **bold** and *italic* prose.

- One
- Two
pre@@@

Sectioning wrappers (@code section code@, @code article code@,
@code header code@, @code footer code@, @code nav code@, @code main code@) are
transparent; comments and scripts are dropped; unknown nodes emit their content
unwrapped; there is no Markdown analog for the opaque container, so it emits just
its body.

@h2 Progressive examples h2@

@ul
@li @strong Fragment vs document. strong@ @@@code renderHtml(expanded) code@@@
gives the bare @code <article> code@;
@@@code renderHtml(expanded, { mode: 'document', title: 'Essay' }) code@@@ gives
a full page. li@
@li @strong Re-theme. strong@ Swap @code css: rawThemeCss code@ for the mechanical
reset, or pass your own string. li@
@li @strong Two targets, one source. strong@ Call @code renderHtml code@ and
@code renderMarkdown code@ on the same @code ExpandedDocument code@. li@
ul@

@h2 Every edge h2@

@ul
@li @code mode code@ default is @code fragment code@ — easy to get wrong. li@
@li @code css: '' code@ yields an unstyled document; the @code <style> code@ tag
is omitted entirely. li@
@li Because @code escapeHtml code@ runs on all text, you never double-escape by
letting the renderer do its job. li@
@li The PDF path (CLI) is just document-mode HTML paginated by headless Chrome —
no renderer difference. li@
ul@

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li Expecting @code document code@ mode by default from @code renderHtml code@. li@
@li Expecting @code renderMarkdown code@ to take options — it takes none. li@
@li Double-escaping — the renderer already escapes; hand it raw strings. li@
@li Worrying about a @@@code </style> code@@@ inside @@@code @@style code@@@ — it
is auto-guarded. li@
ul@

